In JavaScript, the `Array.prototype.sort()` method sorts the elements of an array in a specific order. By default, it sorts in a case-sensitive manner. If you want to sort case-insensitively, you can pass a comparison function as an argument to the `sort()` method. Here's an example:

```javascript
let arr = ['Apple', 'apples', 'banana', 'Banana'];
arr.sort();
console.log(arr); // Output: ['Apple', 'Banana', 'apples', 'banana']

// To sort case-insensitively
arr.sort((a, b) => a.localeCompare(b, undefined, { sensitivity: 'accent' }));
console.log(arr); // Output: ['Apple', 'banana', 'apples', 'Banana']
```

In the example above, the `localeCompare()` method is used to sort the array. The `sensitivity: 'accent'` option makes it case-insensitive. If you want to make it case-insensitive and ignore accents, you can use `'acai'` instead. If you want to ignore accents and make it case-sensitive, you can use `'numeric'`.

For more information, you can refer to the documentation:
- `Array.prototype.sort()`: https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/sort

